WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.390
I have a really sort of bone headed clip to
play for you today from a Republican senator

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:06.860
from Florida, Rick Scott.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:11.119
And the clip is interesting, but really it's
going to serve as a kind of jumping off point

00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:16.680
for us to talk about really some of the basics
of how the American economy is organized and

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:22.700
how it is really based on the type of relationship
that we have with China.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:29.990
And so the point of this is for us to better
understand why Donald Trump's critique of

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:34.840
the U.S. China trade relationship is so wrong
and so misguided.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.760
And you really can't understand that unless
you understand some of what we're going to

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:38.960
talk about here.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.780
So as many of you know, you've probably heard
Trump say, and it's true, the United States

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.290
imports a ton of stuff from China.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:47.620
This includes raw materials.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:52.900
This includes finished electronic products
and other products.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:58.880
And we don't export as much to China as we
import from China, meaning we have a trade

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.820
deficit with China.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:08.620
The obvious reasons why this is the situation
include labor is cheaper in China.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:14.790
The weaker regulatory environment in China
also makes it financially advantageous to

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.090
do this.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.450
That's all true.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:20.650
But there's really a lot more to this that
we have to understand.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:24.040
So to set the conversation, here's Florida
Senator Rick Scott.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:30.380
He's a Republican, of course, saying nobody
in the United States should be buying stuff

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.970
from communist China.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:32.970
Let's look at what he had to say.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:36.790
I believe this was yesterday or the day before
on the floor of the Senate, which prohibits

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:41.430
the U.S. government from purchasing drones
manufactured in countries identified as national

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.650
security threats like Iran and China.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:49.310
We know communist China steals our technology
and intellectual property.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:55.229
Yet the U.S. government continues to buy critical
technology like drones with American tax dollars

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:58.590
from Chinese companies backed by their government.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:03.010
No one in America should buy products made
in communist China, especially not the U.S.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:04.310
government.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.180
We cannot continue this practice.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.619
And my American Security Drone Act is a commonsense
solution to end this threat to our national

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:11.690
security.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:17.099
So what these Republicans and to be honest,
many Democrats get this wrong as well.

00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:23.750
What is being missed about the China trade
conversation so often is that our trade deficit

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:30.099
with China and China holding so much American
debt as they do through bonds.

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:32.349
This is not a bug in the system.

00:02:32.349 --> 00:02:37.150
We might not like it, but it's a feature of
how this system is designed.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.909
And I'll explain to you what that means.

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:46.269
Collectively, the United States decided implicitly,
the United States decided decades ago that

00:02:46.269 --> 00:02:52.540
we value cheap products from China more than
we value domestic production.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.950
We don't want to pay more for stuff that's
made in the United States.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.650
When I say we, you might be okay with it.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:03.799
But as a country in organizing our economy,
our leaders have decided we want the cheap

00:03:03.799 --> 00:03:05.709
stuff from China.

00:03:05.709 --> 00:03:11.419
So our entire economy is based around specialization.

00:03:11.419 --> 00:03:17.559
And this includes exporting services like
we increasingly do to China, but importing

00:03:17.559 --> 00:03:21.069
products, products that have been made in
China because the demand for these products

00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:25.120
is so high and many of these products would
not be affordable to everyday Americans if

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:26.919
they were manufactured in the United States.

00:03:26.919 --> 00:03:30.419
Now, that's because of a whole other set of
problems.

00:03:30.419 --> 00:03:34.590
But if we're going to talk about the U.S.
China trade relationship, we have to sort

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.430
of set this basic understanding.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:41.919
So naturally, this means we're going to import
more stuff from China than we're going to

00:03:41.919 --> 00:03:42.919
export.

00:03:42.919 --> 00:03:44.400
And that's where you get a trade deficit.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:45.650
That's the trade deficit.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:46.780
Trump refers to.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.409
And many Republicans refer to.

00:03:48.409 --> 00:03:50.359
Now, when you hear the word deficit.

00:03:50.359 --> 00:03:51.359
It sounds bad.

00:03:51.359 --> 00:03:55.140
It's meant to sound bad because we think,
oh, deficits, bad, surpluses good.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.680
If you think about your household economy,
your household budget.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:03:59.809
You want to be running a surplus.

00:03:59.809 --> 00:04:03.200
You want to have something left over at the
end of the month rather than than to be deficit

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.010
spending, charging stuff at the end of the
month with the trade relationship.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:11.419
It's not deficit bad surplus good necessarily.

00:04:11.419 --> 00:04:16.449
We run a trade deficit by design because of
how we've organized the economy to depend

00:04:16.449 --> 00:04:18.400
on that cheap stuff from China.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.580
So everybody in the U.S. can afford more stuff
instead of less stuff.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:29.150
And part of what keeps China competitive in
this context is that they set the value of

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:34.039
their currency relative to the dollar using
what's called a modified fixed exchange rate,

00:04:34.039 --> 00:04:40.270
which basically means if the dollar loses
value, China will start buying dollars through

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:41.270
bonds.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:46.220
This is how China ends up holding so much
of our debt because they want to keep that

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.820
exchange rate between the dollar and the yuan
roughly the same.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:55.490
Now, over the last few years, China has reduced
the degree to which they do this.

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:58.669
But part of why China is buying so many bonds
and.

00:04:58.669 --> 00:05:03.639
Holding so much of our debt is because of
this trade relationship that we decided we

00:05:03.639 --> 00:05:08.460
wanted in order to keep the flow of cheap
Chinese stuff coming to the United States.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:13.120
Now, I think it's a good moment to mention
you hear the right.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.280
Always go crazy.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.280
China owns us.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:16.789
China owns our debt.

00:05:16.789 --> 00:05:19.310
Japan actually hold more of our debt than
China.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:22.110
But for political reasons, you don't really
hear much about that.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:27.370
Japan's just not considered a threat in the
same way that China is because their economy

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:29.360
is a different type of economy.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:36.220
But the point here is we as a country, we
as an economy could decide we don't want to

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.099
import stuff from China anymore.

00:05:38.099 --> 00:05:41.970
We don't like the political idea of importing
stuff from China.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.949
We don't like the economic idea, as Rick Scott
said.

00:05:44.949 --> 00:05:47.420
Let's stop buying from communist China.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:49.659
We could do that if we wanted.

00:05:49.659 --> 00:05:56.780
But you have to understand that specialization
and trade, including with China in total,

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:02.540
have created more jobs in the United States
than have been destroyed due to outsourcing.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:09.360
We've given tens of millions of Americans
access to products which we now take for granted.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.030
So I'm not defending it.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.889
What I'm saying is if we want to have the
conversation of no longer having a trade deficit

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:22.770
with China, of no longer buying stuff from
communist China, as Rick Scott says, we can

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:23.770
do that.

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:27.210
It would have massive consequences here in
the United States.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.229
Things would get more expensive.

00:06:28.229 --> 00:06:34.900
A lot of people would lose jobs, but we might
decide that in the long run it's worth it.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:38.909
But if we're going to have this conversation,
we have to have the full conversation because

00:06:38.909 --> 00:06:44.009
we have this fantasy conversation where Trump
says get rid of the trade deficit.

00:06:44.009 --> 00:06:47.659
Rick Scott says don't buy stuff from communist
China.

00:06:47.659 --> 00:06:52.610
And people end up coming away with this idea
that we could just stop buying from China

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:57.039
manufactured domestically and the economy
would be otherwise unaffected.

00:06:57.039 --> 00:07:00.990
Other than we've brought jobs home or whatever
the case may be, that's not the reality.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:03.569
So I'm not saying what would be right or wrong.

00:07:03.569 --> 00:07:08.430
What I'm pointing out is we have decades of
economic policy built upon the foundation

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:15.139
of we value the flow of cheap goods from China
more than we value a lot of other things that

00:07:15.139 --> 00:07:17.639
we could value in the economy.

00:07:17.639 --> 00:07:24.169
Don't allow Republicans like Rick Scott and
Trump and even some Democrats who don't really

00:07:24.169 --> 00:07:25.580
understand this.

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:31.550
Don't let them oversimplify this into stupidity
with let's just end the trade deficit type

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:38.319
of stuff because we have decades of implicit
and explicit decisions built upon.

00:07:38.319 --> 00:07:39.469
We want to keep the free.

00:07:39.469 --> 00:07:40.469
I'm sorry.

00:07:40.469 --> 00:08:56.760
We want to keep the cheap stuff coming from
China.

